    "In the phase‑IIb trial of children 5‑11 years with uncontrolled asthma, the primary endpoint was change from baseline in daily pre‑dose morning (AM) peak expiratory flow (PEF) averaged over the 12‑week treatment period. The intent‑to‑treat (ITT) analysis included 119 participants receiving placebo and 118 receiving fluticasone furoate 50 µg once daily. The least‑squares mean change from baseline was +3.3 L/min (SD 2.63) for placebo and +22.8 L/min (SD 2.65) for FF 50 µg. The between‑group estimate (FF 50 µg vs placebo) was a mean difference of 19.5 L/min with a 95 % confidence interval of 12.1 to 26.9 L/min (p < 0.001)【ClinicalTrials.gov:NCT01563029】.",
    "This comparison means that, after adjusting for baseline PEF and other covariates, children treated with fluticasone furoate 50 µg once daily experienced on average about 20 L/min greater improvement in morning lung function over 12 weeks than those given placebo. The confidence interval indicates that the true adjusted difference is likely between roughly 12 and 27 L/min, supporting a statistically significant benefit of the drug for this outcome."
